<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">West African Vodún</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Vodún, Vodu, vodunsinsen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">West African Vodún is an African traditional religion of the Aja, Ewe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fon-people">Fon peoples</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, with no central authority. It teaches a supreme creator under whom are lesser spirits, the vodúns, who are believed to manifest in shrines and receive offerings, often animal sacrifices; divination, chiefly Fá, is used to gain knowledge from them. While the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>, from about 1727 to 1823, the Fon adopted Fá and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> cults, and kings who introduced new deities were believed to enhance their own power. Enslaved practitioners carried it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, where it shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou</a>. About 17% of Benin's people follow it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-african-vodun.jpg" alt="West African Vodún" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yemi festus · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vodunsi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
